  "assistantText": "{\"verdict\":\"Conditionally acceptable. A pre-existing directory is fine for confidentiality when every key file has a verified protected DACL. Same-user and admin readers stay trusted.\",\"requiredChecks\":[\"Put a protected DACL on the temp file before any key byte, using create-time security or an exclusive handle plus read-back. Fail closed on helper failure.\",\"Semantic compare: protected, DACL present, zero inherited ACEs, owner is the user SID, allows only that SID plus SYSTEM and Administrators.\",\"On one no-follow handle, reject reparse points and extra links, require the same volume, read, and re-check the DACL after rename.\",\"Apply that check to every recovery copy.\"],\"risks\":[\"Same-user processes can read via the user ACE. Handles opened before lockdown keep access. Low integrity can read without NO_READ_UP. SYSTEM, Administrators, and backup privilege can read.\",\"Inherited ACEs remain if the DACL is null, unprotected, or compared as loose SDDL.\",\"Directory tolerance still allows FILE_DELETE_CHILD swaps, a planted junction, and cross-volume rename inheriting the destination ACL.\"],\"windowsOnly\":[\"Real NTFS must show protected owner and DACL, zero inherited ACEs, null-DACL refusal, same-volume versus cross-volume rename, reparse and hard-link refusal, a planted directory, delete-child, low-integrity open, and elevated owner. Script byte parity leaves runtime behavior unproven.\"]}",
